3. Preserve identity and attribution

When distributing the Software or a modified multiplayer framework substantially
based on it, you must retain SixM as the framework's project name and retain
the original copyright, author attribution, and this license.

You may add a descriptive qualifier such as "SixM Community" or "Unofficial
SixM Fork", but you must not replace SixM with another framework brand, strip
its project identity, or present its original work as your own original project.
The SixM name and existing logo must remain identifiable where supplied as
framework branding. These requirements apply only to assets and rights that
the licensor is entitled to license.

This condition concerns the framework's public identity. It does not prevent
ordinary code refactoring, accessibility adaptations, technical file naming,
or choosing names for servers, resources, scripts, and other user content.

4. Identify modified and unofficial versions

Distributed modified versions must clearly identify themselves as modified and
unofficial, describe the material changes, and preserve the original attribution.
Do not imply that a fork, distribution, service, or server is an official SixM
release or is approved by Conrado0x1 without written authorization.

Recipients must receive this license with the Software. Additional terms must
not remove the identity, attribution, or recipient permissions stated here.

